{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2619 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5978dc1a07c8fb579a9189302a0ac6d915b75cc11c5e823c537af34b7a51b330" :edb/db-before-t 2618 :edb/db-after-t 2619 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535931 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:54:16.392Z" 13194139535931 true] [17592186105525 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139535931 false] [17592186105525 1100 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/bd19fd1559a516610f03446ba40c8e0d620575f1ea8b736e9ed4ce07b099253c.png)\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139535931 true] [17592186105525 1145 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/49ca24beba2af93482e8e08a7edd396a2714b17f84decd26199741c728ab1bfa.png)\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=1+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/71e9e1302c0aea79de3370541f0c25acb679e37a5527c172c9490693a4f74c30.png)\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=⟨1+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535931 true] [17592186105525 1145 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3y=1 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 3y = 1.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=1+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1+3y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=⟨1+3y,\\,y⟩,\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535931 false] [17592186105526 1100 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+5y=4 \\\\ 4x+10y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." 13194139535931 false] [17592186105526 1100 "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/13df05563b5770bd877481f24da1c69874d73594e6b82230a2159f01367ba030.png)\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." 13194139535931 true] [17592186105526 1145 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/4aff0484efeeb7e39af2bdb169b6fff07ce8ba9348ff3a4839423bf5c9ead97d.png)\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072159/assets/8a7e8b15bc463b00d805547aa64079a1474b5a4595555a5664fba7bfdfb7f62c.png)\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-8$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139535931 true] [17592186105526 1145 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 4 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{5}{2}y=2 \\\\ 0x+0y=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=-8$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=-8,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139535931 false] [17592186105669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375111 13194139535931 true] [17592186105669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375118 13194139535931 true] [17592186105669 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375125 13194139535931 true] [17592186105670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375104 13194139535931 true] [17592186105670 1020 #edb/ref 17592186375132 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1017 #edb/ref 17592186375105 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1089 "q-20960" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1100 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8x+32y=-40 \\\\ 5x+20y=-25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1113 #uuid "1aae5f9f-6428-5d27-b8a2-86cf26a0a204" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375104 1145 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 32 & -40 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & 32 & -40 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+4y=-5.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-5-4y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-5-4y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375106 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375107 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375108 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375109 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1000 #edb/ref 17592186375110 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1041 "selection" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1104 #edb/ref 17592186375108 13194139535931 true] [17592186375105 1110 #uuid "c6ec63e8-3c68-578a-b514-6bbe727e8dd0" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375106 1034 "No solution" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535931 true] [17592186375106 1111 #uuid "88ea9fc0-fe8a-5659-adca-ffc88484a23f" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375107 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5-3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535931 true] [17592186375107 1111 #uuid "4c556f67-0753-5b7c-a38e-c94576a87078" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375108 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535931 true] [17592186375108 1111 #uuid "1224a1fa-95e5-59ba-982b-b310df917b85" 13194139535931 true] [17592186375109 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\